<!DOCTYPE refentry PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DocBook XML V4.1.2//EN"
          "http://www.oasis-open.org/docbook/xml/4.1.2/docbookx.dtd">
<refentry id="process_denoiseuniform">

	<refmeta>
		<refentrytitle>process_denoiseuniform</refentrytitle>
		<manvolnum>1</manvolnum>
	</refmeta>

	<refnamediv>
		<refname>process_denoiseuniform</refname>
		<refpurpose>remove electrically uniform noise events from spike data files</refpurpose>
	</refnamediv>

	<refsynopsisdiv>
		<cmdsynopsis>
		<command>process_denoiseuniform</command>
		<arg choice="plain"><replaceable>basename</replaceable></arg>
		<arg choice="plain"><replaceable>group</replaceable></arg>
		<arg choice="plain"><replaceable>nChannels</replaceable></arg>
		<arg choice="plain"><replaceable>nSamples</replaceable></arg>
		<arg choice="plain"><replaceable>resolution</replaceable></arg>
		<arg><option>--uniformity-threshold</option> <replaceable>T</replaceable></arg>
		<arg><option>--min-amplitude</option> <replaceable>A</replaceable></arg>
		<arg><option>--dry-run</option></arg>
		<arg><option>--verbose</option></arg>
		</cmdsynopsis>
	</refsynopsisdiv>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Description</title>
		<para>
			<command>process_denoiseuniform</command> identifies and removes
			spike-shaped noise events from a spike dataset. Electrical
			interference artifacts — such as 50/60 Hz mains pickup, cable
			transients, or ground faults — typically appear as waveforms that
			are nearly identical across all channels in a recording group.
			Genuine single-unit action potentials always show a spatially
			varying amplitude profile because the neuron is located
			asymmetrically relative to the electrode sites.
		</para>
		<para>
			For each spike waveform the tool computes a <emphasis>spatial
			uniformity score</emphasis>: the mean standard deviation across
			channels at each sample time, normalised by the overall waveform
			RMS. A score near zero indicates that all channels carry the same
			waveform (noise); a score near one indicates maximal inter-channel
			diversity (genuine spike). Spikes whose score falls below
			<option>--uniformity-threshold</option> are removed.
		</para>
		<para>
			The algorithm operates in two passes. In the first pass all spike
			waveforms are read into memory and classified in parallel
			(OpenMP across spikes when built with <option>-fopenmp</option>).
			In the second pass the filtered timestamps and waveforms are
			written to temporary files which atomically replace the originals
			on success.
		</para>
		<para>
			This tool is normally invoked through the
			<command>ndm_denoiseuniform</command> pipeline script, which
			handles parameter file reading, backup, and multi-group looping.
		</para>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Arguments</title>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>basename</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Session basename without extension (e.g.
						<filename>rat01-20240315-01</filename>).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>group</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Electrode group number (integer, 1-based). The files
						<filename>basename.res.group</filename> and
						<filename>basename.spk.group</filename> are processed.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>nChannels</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Number of recording channels in this electrode group.
						Must be at least 2; single-channel groups cannot have
						a spatial uniformity score computed.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>nSamples</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Number of samples per waveform snippet. Must match the
						value in the session parameter file
						(<literal>spikeDetection/channelGroups/group[N]/nSamples</literal>).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><replaceable>resolution</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						ADC resolution in bits. Currently only 16-bit data is
						supported.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Options</title>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>--uniformity-threshold</option> <replaceable>T</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Spikes with a spatial uniformity score below
						<replaceable>T</replaceable> are classified as noise
						and removed. Default: <literal>0.30</literal>.
						Increase to remove more events; decrease to remove
						fewer. Inspect the score distribution with
						<option>--dry-run --verbose</option> before committing
						to a threshold.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>--min-amplitude</option> <replaceable>A</replaceable></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Spikes whose peak amplitude (in raw ADC counts) is
						below <replaceable>A</replaceable> are retained
						without scoring. They are too small to classify
						reliably and could otherwise be incorrectly discarded.
						Default: <literal>0</literal> (disabled).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>--dry-run</option></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Classify all spikes and report the number that would
						be removed, but do not modify any files. Useful for
						threshold calibration.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><option>--verbose</option></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Print the timestamp, peak amplitude, and uniformity
						score for every spike, with a <literal>[NOISE]</literal>
						marker on those that would be removed.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>File Formats</title>
		<para>
			<command>process_denoiseuniform</command> reads and writes the
			binary spike file formats used by the modern ndmanager-plugins
			pipeline:
		</para>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>.res.N</filename></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Binary, no header. <literal>nSpikes × int64_t</literal>
						sample timestamps (little-endian).
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>.spk.N</filename></term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Binary, no header. <literal>nSpikes × nSamples ×
						nChannels × int16_t</literal>, sample-major layout.
						Element at spike <literal>i</literal>, sample
						<literal>s</literal>, channel <literal>c</literal>:
						byte offset <literal>(i·nSamples·nChannels + s·nChannels + c) × 2</literal>.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>.clu.N</filename> (optional)</term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Plain text. First line: cluster count. Subsequent
						lines: one cluster ID per spike. Filtered in sync
						with <filename>.res</filename> and
						<filename>.spk</filename> when present.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term><filename>.fet.N</filename> (optional)</term>
				<listitem>
					<para>
						Binary. Header: <literal>int32_t nDimensions</literal>.
						Body: <literal>nSpikes × nDimensions × int64_t</literal>,
						row-major. Filtered when present and size-consistent.
					</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Exit Status</title>
		<variablelist>
			<varlistentry>
				<term>0</term>
				<listitem><para>Success.</para></listitem>
			</varlistentry>
			<varlistentry>
				<term>1</term>
				<listitem>
					<para>Error — bad arguments, unreadable input files, or
					I/O failure. Input files are left untouched on error.</para>
				</listitem>
			</varlistentry>
		</variablelist>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Examples</title>
		<para>
			Calibrate the threshold without modifying files:
		</para>
		<screen>process_denoiseuniform session 1 4 32 16 --dry-run --verbose 2>&amp;1 | grep score</screen>
		<para>
			Remove noise with a stricter threshold:
		</para>
		<screen>process_denoiseuniform session 2 4 32 16 --uniformity-threshold 0.40</screen>
		<para>
			Protect very small spikes from being scored:
		</para>
		<screen>process_denoiseuniform session 1 4 32 16 --min-amplitude 200</screen>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>See Also</title>
		<para>
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_denoiseuniform</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_extractspikes</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>,
			<citerefentry><refentrytitle>ndm_pca</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>
		</para>
	</refsect1>

	<refsect1>
		<title>Authors</title>
		<para>neurosuite-3 contributors</para>
	</refsect1>

</refentry>
